WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:11.250
PAUL JAY: Welcome back to Reality Asserts
Itself on The Real News Network.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.940
We're continuing our series of interviews
with John Kiriakou, the former CIA officer

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:22.360
who spent 23 months in jail and, as we record
the interview, has got about 36, 37 days left

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:27.820
[incompr.] house arrest, because--well, really
because he went on television and said that

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:33.960
torture was official U.S. policy. He was charged
with giving a reporter the name of a CIA officer,

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.620
who the reporter actually never published,
and, as far as I understand it, the name was

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.470
out there anyway, but they wanted to get them.
And we'll get into that story more in detail

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:46.230
further into this series. But now we're going
to carry on.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:50.829
We have in the last episode talked a lot about
the Iraq War. About a year before the Iraq

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:54.000
War, more or less, John is posted to Pakistan.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.910
And thanks for joining us.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.920
JOHN C. KIRIAKOU: Good to be here.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.829
JAY: Now, anyone that knows the situation
in Afghanistan knows that--and I don't think

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:10.829
anybody disputes that the Pakistan army intelligence,
the ISI, to a large extent with some Saudi

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:16.049
cooperation and money, helped nurture and
create the Taliban.

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:20.680
KIRIAKOU: Yes. Pakistani intelligence services
created the Taliban with Saudi money.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:21.510
JAY: With Saudi money.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:21.930
KIRIAKOU: Mhm.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:26.830
JAY: And start to spread Wahhabism, which
is not the religious cultural traditions of

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:33.830
Afghanistan. The Taliban and all the links
with al-Qaeda and Osama bin Laden, clearly

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:42.439
the Pakistani intelligence are not only fully
aware that the Taliban works in collaboration

00:01:42.439 --> 00:01:48.659
with the Pakistani ISI. So the Osama bin Laden
in Afghanistan is done in some ways under

00:01:48.659 --> 00:01:51.330
the auspices of the Pakistani intelligence
service.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.799
KIRIAKOU: I always believed that.

00:01:53.799 --> 00:01:59.600
JAY: And I was told that in Kandahar, and
there seemed to be no disputing it.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:06.600
So you go to Pakistan in 2002. You're the
counterterrorism chief CIA official in Pakistan.

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:13.060
So what do you make of this relationship with
Pakistan? The Pakistani military intelligence

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:17.970
is involved in creating the Taliban. They've
got to know about the connections with al-Qaeda

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.650
and bin Laden. We give them, what, I can't
remember the number, but it's hundreds of

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:28.020
millions of dollars in military support. And
it's much--it's somewhat like the Saudi relationship.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.870
But you have, clearly, a dual game going with
[crosstalk]

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:37.950
KIRIAKOU: Absolutely. Absolutely a dual game.
The Pakistanis--that great champion of democracy

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:43.450
and human rights Benazir Bhutto created the
Taliban. And the original reason for creating

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:49.290
the Taliban was to protect Pakistani truck
traffic as it drove across Afghanistan to

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:50.330
deliver goods in Iran.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:51.510
JAY: 'Cause the country was in chaos.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.200
KIRIAKOU: The country was in utter chaos.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:59.300
JAY: As a result of President Carter, Zbigniew
Brzezinski, and Ronald Reagan's policy of

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.849
sucking Russia into a new Vietnam [crosstalk]

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:02.120
KIRIAKOU: Correct.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:02.989
JAY: --destroyed the place.

00:03:02.989 --> 00:03:06.849
KIRIAKOU: That's right. It was literally destroyed.

00:03:06.849 --> 00:03:12.910
So the Taliban began by protecting the trucks.
Then they began setting up posts in villages

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:17.599
along the truck routes. Then they took over
the villages. Then they took over the regions.

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:20.670
Then they took over the states. And the next
thing you know, they took over the whole country.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:24.150
JAY: And from what I know--and I was there
and made a film there and spoke to a lot of

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.379
Afghans--in the early days, it was quite popular,--

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:28.030
KIRIAKOU: It was.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:32.180
JAY: --even amongst secular, modern Afghans
in Kabul, because the place was going to shit.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:36.480
KIRIAKOU: Yes, that's right. And at least
in those early days of the Taliban, people

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:42.909
weren't being killed in shootouts in the street
or having your relatives kidnapped and lynched

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:47.580
because of a property dispute. They really
did bring law and order in those early days

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.550
and they did protect Pakistan's truck traffic.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:56.950
But then everything spun out of control. And
the next thing we know, there's a militant

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:59.090
theocracy in Afghanistan.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:06.090
I believe that even after things went bad
in Afghanistan with the Taliban, that the

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:13.379
Pakistani government continued to support
them, continued to fund them, and continued

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:19.659
to give them safe haven when they needed it,
which led to no end of problems, including,

00:04:19.659 --> 00:04:22.129
probably, the September 11 attacks.

00:04:22.129 --> 00:04:29.129
JAY: So you're now head of counterterrorism
for the CIA in Pakistan. You're working with

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:36.680
Pakistani authorities. And how does this work
within Pakistani authorities? Some of them

00:04:37.229 --> 00:04:42.020
were off here playing footsie with al-Qaeda
and the Taliban, and the other ones are working

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:42.509
with you.

00:04:42.509 --> 00:04:48.069
KIRIAKOU: That's a great question, and the
answer is kind of complicated.

00:04:48.069 --> 00:04:55.069
The Pakistani authorities who were assigned
to work with us: almost entirely Army officers.

00:04:56.139 --> 00:05:03.139
All had been educated in the U.K., trained
in the U.K. or the U.S., solidly pro-American.

00:05:03.729 --> 00:05:10.009
And I can honestly tell you with a completely
straight face that I never asked them for

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:15.800
anything that they didn't give me. Anything
I needed, it was done, and I mean from the

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.990
lowliest private all the way up to the brigadier
general who was in charge of the program.

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:24.379
JAY: Well, not everything. You must have asked
them where bin Laden was. They didn't [crosstalk]

00:05:24.379 --> 00:05:26.719
KIRIAKOU: They didn't--they said they didn't
know.

00:05:26.719 --> 00:05:32.610
Now, with that said, there were certainly
other very powerful elements of the Pakistani

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:39.610
government, and specifically the Pakistani
military, that were solidly pro-Taliban, in

00:05:40.249 --> 00:05:47.249
many cases pro-al-Qaeda, and did not just
nothing to help us, but actively tried to

00:05:48.809 --> 00:05:54.689
stop us from making inroads against al-Qaeda.
Even in the Abu Zubaydah operation, we never

00:05:54.689 --> 00:05:59.849
told the Pakistanis who we were going after.
We called him Mr. Fish, because we had told

00:05:59.849 --> 00:06:01.719
him that he was a big fish.

00:06:01.719 --> 00:06:03.309
So the night that we hit--.

00:06:03.309 --> 00:06:05.909
JAY: Okay, give us some context. We haven't
told that story yet.

00:06:05.909 --> 00:06:11.740
KIRIAKOU: Sure. Just after my arrival in Pakistan,
we got word from headquarters that Abu Zubaydah

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:16.139
was somewhere in the country. And at the time,
we thought Abu Zubaydah was the number-three

00:06:16.139 --> 00:06:21.089
in al-Qaeda. That turned out to not be true.
Not only was he not the number-three (Khalid

00:06:21.089 --> 00:06:25.659
Sheikh Mohammed was the number-three), but
Abu Zubaydah had never actually joined al-Qaeda.

00:06:25.659 --> 00:06:32.249
He created and ran al-Qaeda's two training
camps, but he never pledged fealty to Osama

00:06:32.249 --> 00:06:35.969
bin Laden. He was an independent operator.

00:06:35.969 --> 00:06:42.469
So we were afraid that the people that we
were dealing with on a daily basis would tell

00:06:42.469 --> 00:06:48.499
somebody in their organization who the target
was and that word would get out and Abu Zubaydah

00:06:48.499 --> 00:06:53.879
would be tipped off. So we never told them,
until we actually had him in custody, who

00:06:53.879 --> 00:06:55.569
it was we had been looking for.

00:06:55.569 --> 00:06:58.360
JAY: Where'd the intelligence come from that
he was number three?

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:05.360
KIRIAKOU: Oh, you get bits and pieces from
various human sources. You get liaison sources

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:15.300
reporting to our liaison officers overseas.
You might get some intercepts, press reporting.

00:07:15.909 --> 00:07:20.389
You put everything together, and you try to
come up with this structure for al-Qaeda.

00:07:20.389 --> 00:07:24.969
And the best information we had in 2002 was
that he was number three.

00:07:24.969 --> 00:07:31.969
Don't forget, in February 2002, we didn't
know who Khalid Sheikh Mohammed was. We had

00:07:32.819 --> 00:07:39.309
no idea not only that he was a major player
in al-Qaeda, but that he had masterminded

00:07:39.309 --> 00:07:42.389
the 9/11 attacks. That's how bad the intelligence
was back then.

00:07:42.389 --> 00:07:48.830
JAY: Okay. So you grab this guy. Now, you're
in on organizing the mission to grab this

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:50.509
guy. How does that work?

00:07:50.509 --> 00:07:57.169
KIRIAKOU: We had never hit more than two sites
simultaneously before in Pakistan. We would--my

00:07:57.169 --> 00:08:03.009
first day on the job, I went into the senior
CIA officer's office and I said, what exactly

00:08:03.009 --> 00:08:09.209
do you want me to do here? And he said, I
want you to come up with a modus operandi

00:08:09.209 --> 00:08:14.259
for taking down a terrorist safe house; we've
not done that yet. I said, okay.

00:08:14.259 --> 00:08:20.959
So I remember sitting at my desk with a legal
pad and a pen and thinking, well, if I was

00:08:20.959 --> 00:08:25.309
going to take down a terrorist safe house,
how would I start? Well, I would do it at

00:08:25.309 --> 00:08:29.999
2 a.m., because they would most likely be
asleep by 2 a.m. So I wrote 0200 at the top

00:08:29.999 --> 00:08:36.029
of the paper. And then I just tried to come
up with this step-by-step plan of how I wanted

00:08:36.029 --> 00:08:37.190
to do it.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:40.569
So, finally I went to him and I said, I think
I've come up with a plan; I'm ready to give

00:08:40.569 --> 00:08:44.790
it a try. And he said, well, we've got this
little bit of information that there's this

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:48.350
house. Here's the address. And there might
be an al-Qaeda guy in it. I said, alright,

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:52.910
I'll get a group of guys together, and we're
going to hit it tonight at two, and wish me

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:53.160
luck.

00:08:53.139 --> 00:08:54.089
JAY: And you'd never done this before.

00:08:54.089 --> 00:08:55.740
KIRIAKOU: I had never done this before in
my life.

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:59.269
JAY: I don't understand this. I would have
imagined the CIA had these specialist--take

00:08:59.269 --> 00:09:03.699
down houses specialists, and they fly in,
Jack Bauer shows up.

00:09:03.699 --> 00:09:08.899
KIRIAKOU: Yeah, that all came later, because
of September 11. But before, I mean, it was

00:09:08.899 --> 00:09:10.430
sad sacks like me.

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:14.430
JAY: But CIA's been doing ops for--since they
founded [crosstalk]

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:17.790
KIRIAKOU: Yeah. But not like this, though.
This was different.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:23.930
So I came up with a plan. I got a couple of
the guys together. We called our Pakistani

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:28.730
counterparts. And I said, listen, why don't
we meet up at 12? I'm going to brief you on

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:31.649
this operation that I want us to do tonight.

00:09:31.649 --> 00:09:38.649
So we did. And I planned it so that we were
about a block away from the house at 1:55

00:09:40.829 --> 00:09:45.660
a.m., and then at 1:57 we were going to get
out of the car, and then we were going to

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:50.850
walk to the house. And then, right at 0200--and
we synchronized our watches--we were going

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:55.269
to use a battering ram to break down the door
of the house. The Pakistanis always go in

00:09:55.269 --> 00:10:00.810
first--it's their country. We're going to
grab the guy and take him into custody.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:05.420
So that's what we did. Oh-two-hundred, we
broke down the door with a battering ram,

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:10.329
and we caught this 19-year-old Tunisian kid
who had just escaped across the border from

00:10:10.329 --> 00:10:13.860
Afghanistan and found refuge in this house.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:20.100
So I said, hey, success. Well, let's try it
again in a couple of nights. And the second

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:26.009
time we did it, we caught in Egyptian who
had been a member of Egyptian Islamic Jihad.

00:10:26.009 --> 00:10:30.600
He was actually wanted in Egypt, had been
charged with crimes there, and had escaped

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:35.199
to Afghanistan. And I thought, okay, well,
this is pretty good. Once or twice we did

00:10:35.199 --> 00:10:39.759
two in a night. We would set up two separate
teams and do it. And almost every time we

00:10:39.759 --> 00:10:46.149
did this, we would catch somebody, and they
were almost exclusively low-level nobodies,

00:10:46.149 --> 00:10:51.079
usually teenagers. They would just start to
cry. They wouldn't resist in any way.

00:10:51.079 --> 00:10:52.459
JAY: What would happen to them?

00:10:52.459 --> 00:10:56.870
KIRIAKOU: We would send them, usually, back
to Afghanistan, or in some cases to Guantanamo.

00:10:56.870 --> 00:11:01.779
Remember, the whole purpose of Guantanamo
in those early days was just to have some

00:11:01.779 --> 00:11:06.170
place to put them until we could figure out
where to put them on trial, if they should

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:10.980
be tried in the U.S. or flown back to their
home countries to be tried there. Guantanamo

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:13.350
was never, ever supposed to be a permanent
place.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:17.579
JAY: Some of the people you've picked up and
sent to Guantanamo, you didn't know they'd

00:11:17.579 --> 00:11:19.930
actually done anything, other than they were
in this house.

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:25.730
KIRIAKOU: Correct. Correct. But my position
back then was, if you're an Arab in Pakistan

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:31.779
with no money, no job, no passport, and no
plausible explanation for what you're doing

00:11:31.779 --> 00:11:37.050
there and how you got there, you're al-Qaeda
and you're going to go to Guantanamo until

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:43.060
we can figure out what we are going to do
with you. There were so many people we had

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:50.060
to go after, there was just no time to really
go into things with them, to decide, yes,

00:11:51.189 --> 00:11:54.480
this is really who they are, this is why they're
here.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:59.160
And another thing is many of them, most of
them had been briefed in advance on what to

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:06.160
do and what to say if you're captured. So
everybody told us the same exact story. And

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:13.180
the story was that they had gone to Pakistan
to learn Arabic, they were going to take Arabic

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:18.620
classes, which is odd because nobody speaks
Arabic in Pakistan. They speak Urdu.

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:20.339
JAY: These were mostly Afghans?

00:12:20.339 --> 00:12:21.300
KIRIAKOU: They were all Arabs.

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:24.060
JAY: Well, how could they go learn Arabic?

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:26.139
KIRIAKOU: Precisely.

00:12:26.139 --> 00:12:32.920
So they flew to Karachi. And they were so
happy to have arrived safely in Karachi that

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:37.269
they decided to take a taxi to the grand mosque
to thank God for their safe delivery.

00:12:37.269 --> 00:12:42.439
Well, there is no grand mosque in Karachi.
The grand mosque is in Islamabad.

00:12:42.439 --> 00:12:47.139
And they accidentally, doggone it, left their
passports in the taxi, and that's why they

00:12:47.139 --> 00:12:53.319
have no passport. And so they didn't know
where to stay, where to live. So they went

00:12:53.319 --> 00:12:59.170
to the local Arab coffee shop (there is no
Arab coffee shop in any of these places),

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:04.949
and they met some Arabs there from their own
country, and the Arabs said, oh, not to worry,

00:13:04.949 --> 00:13:11.610
I'll put you in this house, and you wait there
until I can get the embassy to replace your

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:12.410
passport.

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:18.019
Okay? So no matter what country they came
from, they all told exactly the same story.

00:13:18.019 --> 00:13:22.220
JAY: So these were just, you think, young,
naive recruits that didn't [crosstalk]

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:23.680
KIRIAKOU: Yeah. They were nobodies. Kids.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:27.579
JAY: So then you go in and you go after what
you think is the number-three.

00:13:27.579 --> 00:13:31.970
KIRIAKOU: It was more complicated than that.
We got this message from headquarters saying

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:37.639
that Abu Zubaydah was in Pakistan and we had
to catch him. But Pakistan's the size of Texas,

00:13:37.639 --> 00:13:42.509
and it has the population of California. So
to say, just go catch him, it meant nothing

00:13:42.509 --> 00:13:43.589
to me.

00:13:43.589 --> 00:13:49.730
So we flew an analyst in from headquarters,
what was then called a targeting analyst,

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:53.889
somebody who isn't thinking the big thoughts
to write an article in the president's daily

00:13:53.889 --> 00:13:58.920
brief about, you know, a country's next election,
but somebody who's focused on poring through

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:05.920
raw data to try to locate someone who we have
as a target. And it took him about--I'm going

00:14:07.329 --> 00:14:13.240
to say two or three weeks. And finally he
came to me and said, I've narrowed it down,

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:18.699
but only to 14 possible locations. Well, we
had never hit more than two spots before in

00:14:18.699 --> 00:14:25.699
a single night, so the prospect of hitting
14 simultaneously was daunting. I cabled headquarters

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:29.459
and I said, I'm going to need help.

00:14:29.459 --> 00:14:36.459
JAY: So you go after the guy who's number
three and you get the guy, and this begins

00:14:37.259 --> 00:14:42.519
a chain of events which actually winds you
up in jail, really, and you can date that

00:14:42.519 --> 00:14:48.740
chain from probably around here, because some
of your colleagues go off to question this

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:51.430
guy. And what happens?

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:56.519
KIRIAKOU: Abu Zubaydah was very severely wounded
when we captured him. A Pakistani policeman

00:14:56.519 --> 00:15:02.519
shot him three times with an AK-47 and he
nearly died. In fact, the doctor at the emergency

00:15:02.519 --> 00:15:07.149
room where we took him that night told me
that he had never seen wounds so severe where

00:15:07.149 --> 00:15:09.889
the patient lived.

00:15:09.889 --> 00:15:16.889
So we flew Abu Zubaydah to a nearby military
base. And I sat with him for almost 48 hours.

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:23.170
Actually, it was more than 48 hours. I thought
it was 48 hours, and then in discovery in

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:27.550
my case I learned it was 56 hours. So I just
sat at the foot of his bed.

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:33.379
JAY: Just quickly, when John says, "my case",
he means when the CIA charged him and they

00:15:33.379 --> 00:15:38.559
were going through all the back-and-forth
of discoveries that eventually led to his

00:15:38.559 --> 00:15:44.069
conviction, and go back to earlier and you'll
find out he was in jail for 23 months and

00:15:44.069 --> 00:15:47.879
he's now under house arrest, and we're going
to tell this whole story in the next segment

00:15:47.879 --> 00:15:48.129
or two.

00:15:47.980 --> 00:15:54.170
KIRIAKOU: Okay. So I sat with him for 56 hours.
He was in a coma for most of that. He drifted

00:15:54.170 --> 00:15:59.079
out of it a couple of times. At one point,
he asked me to smother him with a pillow.

00:15:59.079 --> 00:16:03.990
He asked me for a glass of red wine, which
I thought was just a delusion.

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:10.990
But then, later on, the next day, he was a
little more aware of what had happened. And

00:16:12.369 --> 00:16:19.369
we talked about the 9/11 attacks briefly.
We talked about Christianity and Islam. He

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:24.550
told me that he enjoyed writing poetry. He
cried. He said he would never know the touch

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:30.850
of a woman, he would never know the joy of
fatherhood. And I urged him repeatedly to

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:37.149
cooperate. And he said no, that he wasn't
going to cooperate. He told me at one point,

00:16:37.149 --> 00:16:42.759
you seem like a nice guy, but you're the enemy,
and I'll never cooperate with the enemy.

00:16:42.759 --> 00:16:46.540
JAY: Did he say why you're the enemy?

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:50.369
KIRIAKOU: Well, I was an American. It was
as simple as that.

00:16:50.369 --> 00:16:55.629
He did say that he didn't want to attack the
United States on September 11, that that was

00:16:55.629 --> 00:17:02.119
bin Laden's decision. He wanted to attack
Israel. But bin Laden overruled him and said

00:17:02.119 --> 00:17:04.410
no, that they were attacking the United States.

00:17:04.410 --> 00:17:09.890
JAY: But he never--there was no ambiguity
or qualification from him that 9/11 [incompr.]

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:16.699
KIRIAKOU: Oh, no, no, no. Not at all. Nine/Eleven
was bin Laden, most definitely.

00:17:16.699 --> 00:17:23.360
So his wounds, like I say, were so severe
he really needed to be treated, formally treated,

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:30.360
not just patched up. So the CIA sent a private
jet with a trauma surgeon from Johns Hopkins

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:38.580
University medical center. They flew to the
military base. And three FBI agents and I

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:46.210
loaded him onto a gurney and carried him up
onto the plane, we laid him across the luggage

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:54.020
rack at the back, and he took off. He went
to a so-called secret location, and I never

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:55.169
saw him again.

00:17:55.169 --> 00:17:58.909
JAY: Now, you heard about the interrogation.
What did you hear?

00:17:58.909 --> 00:18:05.870
KIRIAKOU: Yes. I heard--when I got back to
headquarters, I heard that he had been uncooperative,

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:11.679
that he had been subjected to waterboarding
one time, and that he had cracked. And after

00:18:11.679 --> 00:18:18.390
he cracked, he gave us actionable intelligence
that saved American lives. That turned out

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:25.390
to be a complete lie. He had not been waterboarded
one time; he had been waterboarded 83 times.

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:32.750
In fact, he was cooperative when he was being
interviewed by the FBI. We know from the FBI

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:37.730
agent who was interviewing him that he was
cooperative. And this FBI agent, Ali Soufan,

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:42.320
had made real inroads in this relationship
with Abu Zubaydah.

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:49.320
What happened then was the president ordered
the FBI out, the CIA in, and the CIA began

00:18:49.659 --> 00:18:54.210
torturing him using several different methods
and culminating in waterboarding.

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:57.669
JAY: How do you know it was the president
that ordered it?

00:18:57.669 --> 00:19:02.500
KIRIAKOU: Well, that's what came out over
the course of the investigation later on,

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:08.210
that these tactics had all been approved by
the president and that the White House was

00:19:08.210 --> 00:19:13.720
unhappy with the pace of the interrogations.
They wanted information more quickly.

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:18.600
See, bin Laden said right after September
11 that he was planning another attack that

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:23.419
would dwarf what happened on September 11.
So, many CIA officers were convinced that

00:19:23.419 --> 00:19:27.980
Abu Zubaydah had that information, and they
were determined to get it out of him. That's

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:30.260
why they got rough so quickly.

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:36.950
JAY: There's a lot of discussion in media,
and there was in the political circles and

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:43.750
so on, about torture and the waterboarding
and the Pakistani connection with the Taliban

00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:44.919
and all the rest.

00:19:44.919 --> 00:19:45.510
KIRIAKOU: Mhm.

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:52.260
JAY: But what do you think of the bigger picture,
the overall policy of allying with essentially

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:57.690
what more or less is a military--close to--has
been a military dictatorship in Pakistan,

00:19:57.690 --> 00:20:02.470
and when it's not a straightforward dictatorship,
it's behind-the-scenes military rule just

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:09.470
beneath the sort of political structures,
allying with warlords in Afghanistan, allying

00:20:10.059 --> 00:20:17.059
with a dictatorial monarchy in Saudi Arabia,
Qatar, go on and on and on, that in order

00:20:17.750 --> 00:20:24.750
to maintain America as the hegemon in the
region, we have to--what I said earlier, you

00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:30.350
know, do bad things, supposedly for good reasons,
allied with the worst kind of forces you could

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:31.990
possibly dredge up?

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:37.950
KIRIAKOU: Yes. I think as a country we're
going to have to make a decision at some point,

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:44.210
and that decision's going to have to be whether
we are going to be this beacon of human rights

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:50.440
that we'd like to think we are, this shining
city on a hill that everybody looks up to,

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:57.440
where everybody wants to move, or are we going
to be the biggest, toughest bully on the block,

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:02.450
because we can't be both at the same time.

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:08.100
You know, I happened to be on the State Department's
website the other day, and I clicked on the

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:15.100
link for the Bureau of Democracy, Human Rights,
and Labor. And right at the top of their website,

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:21.740
they have a link to the Universal Declaration
of Human Rights. And Article 5 of that declaration

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:28.529
says that no person shall be subjected to
torture or to humiliating treatment. Okay?

00:21:28.529 --> 00:21:33.929
This is a joke. Congress mandates the State
Department every single year with producing

00:21:33.929 --> 00:21:39.899
a human rights report on every single country
with which we have diplomatic relations.

00:21:39.899 --> 00:21:45.990
I was the embassy human rights officer for
two years in Bahrain when I was living there,

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:52.990
and I can tell you that when you go in to
a host government's foreign ministry and you

00:21:53.919 --> 00:22:00.769
dress them down for disrespecting human rights,
but then you have a CIA officer going to see

00:22:00.769 --> 00:22:07.769
his counterpart in the afternoon and telling
him, telling the foreign intelligence counterpart,

00:22:08.029 --> 00:22:11.830
we want you to take this prisoner and we want
you to really lean on him, but just be sure

00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:17.830
to give us a transcript of what he says, you
completely undercut your entire policy.

00:22:17.830 --> 00:22:24.830
So, while we have these laws that are supposed
to protect human rights and supposed to mandate

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:33.820
the protection of human rights overseas, it's
really little more than a bad joke, because

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:40.370
we don't respect human rights. I mean, we
had, under George W. Bush, our own Justice

00:22:40.370 --> 00:22:47.370
Department doing back flips to try to figure
out how to legally justify torture. And they

00:22:47.419 --> 00:22:54.419
contorted themselves in such a way that they
did endorse torture. And we go through life

00:22:56.140 --> 00:23:01.890
saying, okay, well, then, if the Justice Department
said it's right, then it must be right, so

00:23:01.890 --> 00:23:04.919
we can go ahead and torture. It makes no sense.

00:23:04.919 --> 00:23:07.140
JAY: It's been nonstop since World War II.

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:09.269
KIRIAKOU: It's been nonstop since World War
II.

00:23:09.269 --> 00:23:14.490
You know, I'll tell you another thing. In
the course of my research since I've left

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:19.409
the agency, I've found a picture that ran
on the front page of The Washington Post in

00:23:19.409 --> 00:23:25.120
1968, and it was a picture of an American
soldier waterboarding a North Vietnamese prisoner.

00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:31.750
When that picture was published, the Pentagon
began an investigation, they court-martialed

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:37.620
the soldier, they charged him with torture,
they convicted him, and they jailed him. So

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:44.620
why was torture wrong in 1968, but then it's
right in 2002 and it's right in 2015 and nobody's

00:23:46.519 --> 00:23:52.700
prosecuted for committing torture? It's the
ultimate in hypocrisy to me.

00:23:52.700 --> 00:23:54.200
JAY: Alright. Thanks for joining us.

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:54.799
KIRIAKOU: Thanks for having me.

00:23:54.799 --> 00:23:58.820
JAY: We're going to continue our series of
interviews with John Kiriakou on Reality Asserts

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:05.820
Itself on The Real News Network.

